3. WHAT THE TIE-BREAK COSTS. Signed bias in price units, positive
   means the buyer pays less than the exact midpoint.
   crossing books: 15526
   rule                         volume lost    mean signed bias
   lowest maximiser                       0              8.8306
   highest maximiser                      0             -8.8306
   midpoint, floored                      0              0.1429

4. WHERE THE ROUNDING GOES. Absolute error against exact
   arithmetic, in minor units, over every non-zero fill. Swept over
   lot size, because the answer is not a constant factor.
      max lot    fills   round-first mean      max   divide-once mean    max    ratio
          100    11387               6.12     50.0              0.074    0.5      82x
         1000    11249              57.58    499.0              0.071    0.5     807x
        10000    11387             609.94   5000.0              0.076    0.5    8011x
       100000    11361            6188.31  49949.0              0.076    0.5   81278x
   The round-first error grows with the lot. The divide-once error is
   bounded by one half whatever the lot is. That is the invariant, and
   any single 'improvement factor' is an artefact of the lot size the
   benchmark happened to draw.

